        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Most damp basements don't need excavation or a drainage system — they need bare walls, sealed cracks, and two proper coats of a masonry waterproofing coating. Confirm you're dealing with dampness rather than active seepage first, do the prep work properly, and interior damp-proofing will solve the problem for a fraction of what a full waterproofing system costs.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-4">
            The projects that go wrong are almost never the ones where someone follows this sequence patiently. They're the ones where a homeowner skips the foil test and coats over an active pressure leak, or rushes the prep and paints straight over old paint and efflorescence. Take the diagnosis step seriously, respect the drying and cure times at every stage, and a weekend of real work will keep your basement dry for a decade or more.
